  "type": "Proposition",
  "number": "3.1",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  The ring is a complete uniform non-noetherian Tate -algebra and an integral domain. Moreover,    Lean (finiteJet_isUniform)  Lean (finiteJet_isDomain)  Lean (finiteJet_not_noetherian)  Lean (isClosed_jetSupport)  Lean (isTateRing_JetA)  Lean (finiteJet_isUniform_of_dvr)  Lean (isPowerBounded_JetA_iff)  Lean (finiteJet_isDomain)  Lean (finiteJet_not_noetherian)   The Gauss norm on is multiplicative, and the restricted Gauss norm on is therefore multiplicative as well. In particular, is a domain, and hence so is its subring . The constant- and linear-coefficient maps are continuous, and is closed in for the Gauss norm. Hence the condition that these two coefficients lie in defines a closed subring of , so is complete. The image of in is a topologically nilpotent unit, and therefore is a Tate ring.  The coefficient description of shows that it is exactly the unit ball for the restricted Gauss norm on . Since this norm is multiplicative, It follows that is power-bounded if and only if . Thus , and the unit ball is bounded, so is uniform.  To see that is not noetherian, let . If were generated by , write for the coefficient of in . Now, for every , we can write and write for the constant coefficient of as a series in . By , we have . Since , its coefficients in degrees and vanish. The coefficient of in is therefore exactly . Comparing the coefficients of on both sides gives Thus would generate as a -module. This is impossible. Indeed, it would make integral over . Multiplying a monic relation for of degree by would put in the proper ideal . Thus is not finitely generated, and is not noetherian.   "
