  "type": "Proposition",
  "number": "5.3",
  "title": "",
  "body": "  For every unit-ideal datum as above, the canonical sequence of graph quotients is strict exact, and is a strict surjection. Equivalently,    Lean (loc_row_exact)  Lean (loc_pair_isEmbedding)  Lean (locψC_lipschitz)  Lean (locψC_surjective)  Lean (locψC_isOpenMap)  Lean (bridgeFwd_natural_B)  Lean (bridgeFwd_natural_C)  Lean (bridgeFwd_natural_legB)  Lean (bridgeFwd_natural_legC)  Lean (valueRow_injective)  Lean (valueRow_glue)  Lean (valueRow_embedding)  Lean (loc_row_exact)  Lean (loc_pair_isEmbedding)  Lean (locRhoC_surjective)  Lean (locRhoC_continuous)  Lean (locRhoC_isOpenMap)  Lean (presheafValueMapC_restriction)  Lean (presheafValueMapB_restriction)   The ideals are closed by , and is closed by . Thus   We first prove algebraic exactness. If a representative maps into both and , the pullback assertion in gives an element of with the same two images. Injectivity of then shows that . This gives injectivity on the left. Conversely, take compatible classes in and and choose representatives and . Their defect in belongs to . Lift this defect to an element of using the strict surjection in , and add it to . The corrected representatives agree in , so lifts them to . This proves algebraic exactness.  For the topology on the left, let and put Choose representatives and whose norms are arbitrarily close to the quotient norms of and . Their defect lies in . The norm estimate for proved in gives a constant and a correction mapping to this defect such that Thus and agree in and lift to . The max-norm identity for the ambient pullback gives, after letting the error in the representatives tend to zero, for a constant depending only on the datum. The two quotient maps are norm-nonincreasing, so this estimate says precisely that is a topological embedding.  The coefficientwise section shows directly that is surjective. More precisely, choose a representative in whose norm is arbitrarily close to the quotient norm of a given class in , and apply the norm-preserving section. Its class in has no larger norm. Thus the surjection is open; together with continuity, this says that it is strict.   "
